The orthogonal projection matrix
Once we have the projection matrix for \(\mathcal{S}\), we can easily form the projection matrix onto the set’s orthogonal complement, \(\mathcal{S}^\perp\):
\[
\underset{\mathcal{S}^\perp}{\boldsymbol{P}} = \boldsymbol{I}- \underset{\mathcal{S}}{\boldsymbol{P}}.
\]
One way to see this is via the identity between left and right inverses. By orthogonality,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{U}&
\boldsymbol{U}^\perp
\end{pmatrix}^\intercal
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{U}&
\boldsymbol{U}^\perp
\end{pmatrix} = \boldsymbol{I}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Then, by the equivalence between left and right inverses,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\boldsymbol{I}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{U}&
\boldsymbol{U}^\perp
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{U}&
\boldsymbol{U}^\perp
\end{pmatrix}^\intercal
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{U}&
\boldsymbol{U}^\perp
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{U}^\intercal\\
(\boldsymbol{U}^\perp)^\intercal
\end{pmatrix}
\end{aligned}
=
\boldsymbol{U}\boldsymbol{U}^\intercal+ \boldsymbol{U}^\perp (\boldsymbol{U}^\perp)^\intercal.
\]
Solving gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\underset{\mathcal{S}^\perp}{\boldsymbol{P}} = \boldsymbol{U}^\perp (\boldsymbol{U}^\perp)^\intercal= \boldsymbol{I}- \boldsymbol{U}\boldsymbol{U}^\intercal= \boldsymbol{I}- \underset{\mathcal{S}}{\boldsymbol{P}}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Redundant regressors and zero eigenvalues
In fact, \(\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\) is invertible precisely when \(\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\) has a zero eigenvalue. In the preceding example, we can see that
\[
\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}
\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\ -1
\end{pmatrix}
=
N \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 3  \\
3 & 9  \\
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\ -1
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\ 0
\end{pmatrix},
\]
so \((3, -1)^\intercal\) is a zero eigenvector. (In general you might find this by numerical eigenvalue decomposition, but in this case you can just guess the zero eigenvalue.)
Going back to Equation 4, we see that this means that
\[
\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{Y}=
(\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X})
\begin{pmatrix}
\hat{\alpha}\\ \hat{\gamma}
\end{pmatrix}
=
N \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 3  \\
3 & 9  \\
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\hat{\alpha}\\ \hat{\gamma}
\end{pmatrix}
=
N \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 3  \\
3 & 9  \\
\end{pmatrix}
\left(
\begin{pmatrix}
\hat{\alpha}\\ \hat{\gamma}
\end{pmatrix}
+ C
\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\ -1
\end{pmatrix}
\right)
\]
for any value of \(C\). This means there are an infinite set of “optimal” values, all of which set the gradient of the loss to zero, and all of which have the same value of the loss function (i.e. acheive the same fit). And you can check that these family of values are exactly the ones that satisfy \(\alpha + 3 \gamma = \hat{\beta}= \bar{y}\), since
\[
\alpha + 3 \gamma =
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 3
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\alpha \\ \gamma
\end{pmatrix}
\quad\quad\textrm{and}\quad\quad
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 3
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\ -1
\end{pmatrix} = 0.
\]
Soon, we will see that this is a general result: when \(\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\) is not invertible, that means there are many equivalent least squares fits, all characterized precisely by the zero eigenvectors of \(\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\).

Redundant regressors and zero eigenvalues
Suppose we run the (silly) regression \(y\sim \alpha \cdot 1 + \gamma \cdot 3 + \varepsilon_n\). That is, we regress on both the constant \(1\) and the constant \(3\). We have
\[
\boldsymbol{X}=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 3 \\
1 & 3 \\
1 & 3 \\
\vdots
\end{pmatrix}
=
(\boldsymbol{1}\quad 3 \boldsymbol{1})
\]
and so
\[
\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}=
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{1}^\intercal\boldsymbol{1}& 3 \boldsymbol{1}^\intercal\boldsymbol{1}\\
3 \boldsymbol{1}^\intercal\boldsymbol{1}& 9 \boldsymbol{1}^\intercal\boldsymbol{1}\\
\end{pmatrix}
=
N \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 3  \\
3 & 9  \\
\end{pmatrix}
\]
This is not invertible (the second row is \(3\) times the first, and the determinant is \(9 - 3 \cdot 3 = 0\)). So \(\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\) is not defined. What went wrong?
One way to see this is to define \(\beta = \alpha + 3 \gamma\) and write
\[
y_n = (\alpha + 3 \gamma) + \varepsilon_n = \beta + \varepsilon_n.
\]
There is obviously only one \(\hat{\beta}\) that minimizes \(\sum_{n=1}^N\varepsilon_n^2\), \(\hat{\beta}= \bar{y}\). But there are an infinite set of choices for \(\alpha\) and \(\gamma\) satisfying
\[
\alpha + 3 \gamma = \hat{\beta}= \bar{y}.
\]
Specifically, for any value of \(\gamma\) we can take \(\alpha = \bar{y}- 3 \gamma\), leaving \(\beta\) unchanged. All of these choices for \(\alpha,\gamma\) acheive the same \(\sum_{n=1}^N\varepsilon_n^2\)! So the least squares criterion cannot distinguish among them.
In general, this is what it means for \(\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\) to be non–invertibile. It happens precisely when there are redundant regressors, and many regression coefficients that result in the same fit.

