Zero variance regressors
An example of redundant regressors occurs when the sample variance of \(x_n\) is zero and a constant is included in the regression. Specifically, suppose that \(\overline{xx} - \overline{x}^2 = 0\).






Exercise



Prove that \(\overline{xx} - \overline{x}^2 = 0\) means \(x_n\) is a constant with \(x_n = \bar{x}\). Hint: look at the sample variance of \(x_n\).


Let’s regress \(y_n \sim \beta_1 + \beta_2 x_n\).
For simplicity, let’s take \(x_n = 3\). In that case we can rewrite our estimating equation as
\[
y_n = \beta_1 + \beta_2 x_n + \varepsilon_n
     = (\beta_1 + \beta_2 \bar{x}) + \varepsilon_n.
\]
We’re thus in the previous setting with \(\bar{x}\) in place of the number \(3\).

Probabilistic interpretation (bonus content)
We showed in last lecture that if we have a regressor matrix \(\boldsymbol{Z}\) such that the entries of \(\boldsymbol{Z}\) are IID, mean zero, uncorrelated, and unit variance, then by the LLN,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{N} \boldsymbol{Z}^\intercal\boldsymbol{Z}\rightarrow \boldsymbol{I}.
\end{aligned}
\]
For a general \(\boldsymbol{X}\), the rows \(\boldsymbol{x}_n\) are of course not uncorrelated, mean zero, nor unit variance. However, if we assume that the rows are independent, we can de-correlated them if we know the second moment matrix \(\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}:= \mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{x}_n \boldsymbol{x}_n^\intercal\right]\). (Note that \(\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}\) is not a covariance unless \(\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{x}_n\right] = \boldsymbol{0}\).) If we define
\[
\boldsymbol{z}_n = \boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2} \boldsymbol{x}_n,
\]
then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{z}_n \boldsymbol{z}_n^\intercal\right]
={}&
\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2} \boldsymbol{x}_n \boldsymbol{x}_n^\intercal\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2}\right]
& \textrm{(note that }\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2}\textrm{ is symmetric)}
\\={}&
\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2} \mathbb{E}\left[ \boldsymbol{x}_n \boldsymbol{x}_n^\intercal\right] \boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2}
& \textrm{(note that }\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2}\textrm{ is not random)}
\\={}&
\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2} \boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2}
\\={}&
(\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2} \boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{1/2}) (\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{1/2} \boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}^{-1/2})
\\={}&
\boldsymbol{I}.
\end{aligned}
\]
That is, \(\boldsymbol{z}_n\) are almost like ``de–correlated’’ versions of \(\boldsymbol{x}_n\), except for the fact that \(\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{z}_n \boldsymbol{z}_n^\intercal\right]\) is a second moment matrix, not a correlation matrix, because \(\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{z}_n\right] \ne \boldsymbol{0}\).
We don’t know \(\boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}\), of course, but note that
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{N} \boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\rightarrow \boldsymbol{M}_{\boldsymbol{X}}
\end{aligned}
\]
by the LLN. So by regressing on
\[
\begin{aligned}
\boldsymbol{u}_n :=  \left( \frac{1}{N} \boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\right)^{-1/2} \boldsymbol{x}_n,
\end{aligned}
\]
we are regressing on a new linear combination of regressors whose second moment matrix should be approximately \(\boldsymbol{I}\) for large \(N\). (Note that you cannot apply the LLN to \(\boldsymbol{u}_n\), since each entry depends on all the entires in \(\boldsymbol{X}\), and so \(\boldsymbol{u}_n\) are not independent.) The regression on \(\boldsymbol{U}\) gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\boldsymbol{U}={}& \boldsymbol{X}\left( \frac{1}{N} \boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\right)^{-1/2} \\
\boldsymbol{U}^\intercal\boldsymbol{U}={}& N \left( \frac{1}{N} \boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\right)^{-1/2}
\frac{1}{N} \boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\left( \frac{1}{N} \boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\boldsymbol{X}\right)^{-1/2} \\
={}& N \boldsymbol{I}.
\end{aligned}
\]
So \(\boldsymbol{U}\) is in fact exactly an orthogonal design matrix.
